  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 101 AM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nAs we look towards the weekend and into the beginning of next week, \nupper level ridging will build into the western US, while troughing \ndeepens over the eastern US and western Atlantic. This will allow a \nseries of fronts to drop down from the northern Plains towards the \nSoutheast US coast before dissipating. A stream of enhanced deeper \nlevel moisture will set up across South FL the end of this weekend \ninto next week as it gets squeezed between the trough in the eastern \nUS and high pressure in the central Atlantic. Another noticeable \ndifference during this time period is that the low level flow across \nSouth FL will become more westerly starting Sunday and lasting \nthrough at least the early part of next week. This will bring the \nfocus of convection each afternoon into early evening more into the \neast coast metro, resulting in higher than normal PoPs each day. \nWhile the rainfall is certainly welcome news given the ongoing \ndrought conditions across the area, will need to be on the lookout \nfor localized urban and poor drainage flooding each day. \n\nTemperatures during the extended period will remain above normal, \nwith highs each day generally in the low to mid 90s. Heat indices of \n105-110 will be possible each day, but with the increased rainfall \nchances it'll be tough to reach criteria for at least 2 hours each \nday, so any hazard decisions will likely be made on a day by day \nbasis.",
